# The root project intentionally ignores JSON/TXT data files broadly. Desktop
# keeps its manifests and metadata reviewable.
#
# trusted-update-keys.json used to be ignored here, on the reading that it was
# "the release key". It is the PUBLIC half: it ships inside every installer, so
# it is not a secret, and keeping it out of the repository meant a release could
# only be built on a machine that happened to have a copy. CI could not build a
# correct one at all -- it would fall back to the placeholder in the .example
# file and ship an anchor that trusts nothing. The private key stays out of the
# repository, which is what that rule was actually for.
!**/*.json
!**/*.txt

frontend/node_modules/
frontend/.next/
frontend/out/
frontend/*.tsbuildinfo
backend/tests/.tmp-*/
**/dist/
**/build/
installer/output/
